1) **capacitor.config.ts:** add `plugins: { SystemBars: { initialViewportFitValueHint: 'cover' } }`. This matches index.html and avoids a layout jump on the first insets pass. Then run `npm run android:sync` so android/app/src/main/assets/capacitor.config.json picks it up.

2) **Status and navigation bar styles:** in src/platform.ts or src/main.tsx, add the code below. It only runs in the APK and only acts when the WebView actually extends under the status bar. That guard keeps the old-WebView fallback, where the decor view is padded and its background is light, on DEFAULT.

```ts
import { SystemBars, SystemBarsStyle, SystemBarType } from '@capacitor/core';

if (isNative()) {
  const probe = document.createElement('div');
  probe.style.cssText = 'position:fixed;top:0;left:0;width:0;visibility:hidden;pointer-events:none;height:env(safe-area-inset-top)';
  document.body.appendChild(probe);
  let last = '';
  const apply = () => {
    const underBar = probe.getBoundingClientRect().height > 0;
    const key = String(underBar);
    if (key === last) return;
    last = key;
    SystemBars.setStyle({ bar: SystemBarType.StatusBar, style: underBar ? SystemBarsStyle.Dark : SystemBarsStyle.Default }).catch(() => {});
    // The page is always light (no dark theme), so the navigation bar always gets dark icons.
    SystemBars.setStyle({ bar: SystemBarType.NavigationBar, style: SystemBarsStyle.Light }).catch(() => {});
  };
  new ResizeObserver(apply).observe(probe);
  apply();
}
```

The plugin re-applies these stored styles on configuration changes (handleOnConfigurationChanged), so they survive rotation and dark-mode switches. If a dark CSS theme is added later, make the navigation-bar style follow it.

3) **src/ui/styles.css, modal backdrop and dialog:** keep the existing lines as fallbacks and add the safe-area versions after them.

```css
